<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Existentialism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as L'existentialisme</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Existentialism is a family of philosophical views that explore the individual's struggle to lead an authentic life despite the apparent absurdity of existence, stressing personal freedom, responsibility and choice. Its 19th-century forerunners include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soren-kierkegaard">Søren Kierkegaard</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-nietzsche">Friedrich Nietzsche</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fyodor-dostoevsky">Fyodor Dostoevsky</a>, who criticised rationalism. The term was coined by Gabriel Marcel in the mid-1940s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-paul-sartre">Jean-Paul Sartre</a> adopted it in a lecture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1945, published as Existentialism Is a Humanism. The label is most associated with Sartre, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-heidegger">Martin Heidegger</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simone-de-beauvoir">Simone de Beauvoir</a>, Karl Jaspers and, more controversially, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-camus">Albert Camus</a>, and it influenced theology, drama, literature and psychology.</p></div>
